WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
On the Home screen, click New project.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
The project opens on a blank page: click From template.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
In the Color tab, click Color full.

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Click Add lights and choose the 580 lights: nothing else, no darks and no flats.

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Click Open frames: the Darks and Flats zones read none, optional.

6
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Click the Calibration step.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:28.000
In the Darks tab, the window says what Calibration does without darks.

8
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
In the Flats tab, it says what stays in the image without flats.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Click the Session 1 step, then the Sessions tab.

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
The Checks list says it too: no darks, no flats, and the black level used in their place.

11
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Click Graph to go back to the workflow.

12
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Click the project block and type NGC 7000 in Target: the position is found.

13
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Click Run. The button gives the time the run should take.

14
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Name the project NGC 7000, pick its folder with Browse…, then click Save.

15
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
The run starts: it lists its blocks and the time each should take.

16
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
On the graph, each block shows its progress.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Not shown in real time: the whole run took 46 min 44 s on a graphics card (RTX 4060 Ti).

18
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Click Inspect, then the Calibration step.

19
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:22.000
The Calibration measures give what was done instead: the hot pixels found in the lights, the black level used as dark, no flat divided.

20
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Click Post.

21
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Post shows the image as the workflow gives it. Before any gesture, look at what is wrong, and what is already right.

22
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.000
At 100 %, the corners fall to near black and the edges are black: Crop will take them off.

23
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:43.000
The sky is cyan-green, the dark lanes look teal: Dehaze and a Color balance under a mask will correct it.

24
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:51.000
The sky is lighter around the centre, a veil the flats would have removed: Dehaze will lift it. The nebula looks a little flat: Contrast and Faint tones will bring it out.

25
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Over the Milky Way, the stars are dense and cover the gas: the star sliders will quieten them.

26
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Already right, to leave alone: the red of the nebula and its structure, the colour of the stars, and the noise, which Denoise has done.

27
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Click Crop.

28
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Drag the top-left handle in, then the bottom-right one: about 5080 × 3600 px, NGC 7000 and the Pelican in the frame.

29
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Click Apply.

30
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.000
Drag Contrast to the right until the Wall and the Pelican’s edge stand out: here +0.8.

31
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.000
Drag Faint tones to the right until the faint gas comes up: here +0.6.

32
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Click the Starless image row.

33
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.000
Click Filter, then Contrast, then Dehaze.

34
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Drag Amount until the sky’s light veil is gone without swallowing the faint gas: here about 0.25.

35
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Leave Scale wide so that only the large veil is taken: here about 256 px.

36
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Click Filter, then Color, then Color balance.

37
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Drag Temperature to the right and Green / magenta to the left, to the ends of the sliders: +1 and −1.

38
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000
On the whole image the same balance warms the nebula too: it must act on the dark tones only.

39
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Click the mask button, then Add to mask, then Luminance range.

40
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Set the range from 0 to about 22 %, so that only the sky is in the mask.

41
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Soften the mask edge with the feather: here about 8 px.

42
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Under the mask, the sky is neutral and the nebula keeps its red.

43
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.000
A little green remains: click Filter, then Color, then Green removal.

44
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Drag Amount until the sky has no green left: here about 0.6.

45
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.000
The mask is not copied from one filter to the next: draw it again the same way, with the mask button, Add to mask, Luminance range, from 0 to 22 %, feather 8.

46
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Set the range from 0 to about 22 %, so that only the sky is in the mask.

47
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.000
Soften the mask edge with the feather: here about 8 px.

48
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.000
With both filters under the mask, the sky is neutral and the nebula keeps its red.

49
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.000
Click the top layer, then drag the star sliders gently: Strength −0.5, Small stars −1.0, Star size −0.5, Star color +0.3.

50
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:32.000
The dense star field lets the gas show through.

51
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Click Export…

52
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.000
Choose JPEG in Format.

53
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Check the file, on the tutorial drive, then click Export.

54
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Click Export.

55
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
The image is written, with its processing history beside it.

56
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Save the project with Ctrl+S.
